-- **`bibliothek/linux` -- the POSIX primitives a HOSTED Gabbro program binds,
-- as ordinary user code** (server lane, TODO section 0e K8, 2026-09-28).
--
-- WHAT THIS IS. Simon's binding constraint (2026-09-27) is *"API calls are
-- always user-made"*, and on 2026-09-28 he drew it for the runtimes too: *"an
-- die Hardware ist OK, OS nicht, das muss selbst gemacht werden"*. So a Gabbro
-- program reaches an operating system only through items IT declares -- with
-- their ABI, their effects, their costs and the assumption their bodies keep --
-- and that now holds for the runtime beside it as well.
--
-- `bibliothek/linux-kmod` is the same file for the Linux KERNEL MODULE target,
-- and it was written first (K7). This one is its hosted twin, deliberately in
-- the same order and with the same three rules, so that the two can be read
-- against each other. **A different operating system gets a different pair of
-- files here, not a different Gabbro.**
--
-- WHAT IT COVERS TODAY, and the boundary is measured rather than asserted.
-- `instrumente/pruefe-os-bindung.sh` reads what the hosted runtime still pulls
-- out of the OS -- `nm -u` over a built binary, intersected with the runtime's
-- own objects, so the program's own calls do not count -- and its mark is the
-- worklist. This file binds the whole of the BOUNDED HEAP
-- (`laufzeit/arena_dyn.c`): the reservation, the commit, the page size, and the
-- two halves of a fail-stop; and, since the second slice, the whole of the
-- GENERATED DRIVER (`<unit>.treiber.c`): its threads, its locks, and the words
-- of its two error paths. **The symbol mark is 0 with these eleven bound** --
-- the hosted runtime names no operating-system function of its own any more.
-- What is left is the raw `clone`/`futex` of `laufzeit/faden.c`, which leaves
-- no symbol at all and is counted as SITES by the instrument's second stage
-- (`MARKE_ROHRUF`).
--
-- WHAT A PROGRAM DOES WITH IT. Two lines in the manifest of a unit that
-- declares an `arena`, a `lock` or a `concurrent` set:
--
--     unit driver program
--       my-program.gab
--       bibliothek/linux/linux.gab   -- these declarations
--       bibliothek/linux/linux.c     -- their bodies
--
-- and `gabbro build` REFUSES a hosted unit that names neither, with the missing
-- declaration and the file that supplies it (`crates/gabbro-cli/src/bau.rs`,
-- `bindungsregel_gehostet`). *Never a silent default* -- and never a link error
-- either, which is what the refusal buys: the linker's word would be
-- `gabbro_os_reserve undefined`, about a name the program never wrote.
--
-- **The demand grows with the use, per thing the unit has**: an `arena` asks for
-- the three storage calls, a `lock` for the three lock calls, a `concurrent` set
-- for the two thread calls, and any of the three for the report channel. A unit
-- with none of them is refused nothing, because the runtime beside it calls
-- nothing.
--
-- **WHY THERE IS NO `use` LINE**, the same answer as the module binding's:
-- nothing in a program CALLS these items, the runtime does. A `use` would name
-- an item the program never mentions again -- ceremony documenting a dependency
-- the manifest already carries. What makes the declarations load-bearing is the
-- pair of checks around them: `gabbro build` refuses a unit that lacks them, and
-- the C compiler holds each one against `laufzeit/bindung.h` inside the
-- runtime's own translation unit, so a wrong type is a build error.
--
-- WHY EVERY ADDRESS IS A `u64`. Gabbro has no pointer type -- the same reason a
-- system-call binding passes registers (Opus agent L, OFFEN O31). The
-- reservation's base crosses as a number and the two sides cast: the runtime
-- with `(uint64_t)(uintptr_t)`, the bodies in `linux.c` back again. Both halves
-- stand beside each other so that neither can drift.
--
-- WHAT THE `costs` CLAUSES SAY, AND WHAT THEY DO NOT. Each one is the
-- primitive's OWN work once it runs. **Waiting is not in it, and neither is the
-- kernel's**: a `mmap` of 32 GiB of `PROT_NONE` walks page tables the program
-- cannot count, and waiting bounds are NOT CLAIMED anyway
-- (`grammatik/Grammatik/Zielsatz/Spec.lean`, the header's list). The numbers
-- below are therefore the same kind of statement as the module binding's -- a
-- declared bound on the call itself, which is what a `costs` clause is about.
module linux::hosted {

-- **The ABI and the behaviour of all six bodies, as one named assumption.** One
-- and not six, because it is one fact about one file: `bibliothek/linux/linux.c`
-- is compiled into the program's own build, with the program's own flags,
-- against the C library of the machine it is built for.
assume os_bindung_abi
    "Every body in `bibliothek/linux/linux.c` keeps the x86_64 SysV convention of its declaration here, answers 0 exactly where the declaration says a refusal is 0, and `gabbro_os_ende` never returns."
    falsifier sonde_os_bindung;

-- **What the thread and lock bodies keep, and it is the whole of what the goal
-- theorem asks of the environment under assumption (d).** A POSIX mutex is a
-- mutual-exclusion lock and `pthread_join` returns after the root's last step;
-- the Lean statement's `Laufzeit` says exactly that about the declared starts
-- and the lock moves, and nothing here re-proves it. It is named apart from the
-- ABI because a binding could keep every calling convention and still hand back
-- a RECURSIVE mutex or a `pthread_create` that ran the body on the calling
-- thread -- both of which type-check, link, and break race freedom in silence.
assume os_bindung_faden
    "`gabbro_os_sperre_nimm`/`_gib` are mutual exclusion over the blob they are given -- at most one thread holds it and a release is visible to the next holder; `gabbro_os_faden_start` runs its root ONCE on a thread of its own and answers non-zero if it did not start; `gabbro_os_faden_warte` returns only after that root's last step."
    falsifier sonde_os_faden;

-- **The three promises the run-time `start` rests on, and their ORDER is the
-- whole of it.** `laufzeit/faden.c` is a LOOP over a join word and nothing else;
-- every step it takes is a reading of this assumption. It is named apart from
-- the pair above because the primitive is a different one -- a raw `clone` and
-- not a `pthread_create` -- and because the middle clause is what was once
-- false: until 2026-09-26 the id was stored AFTER the thread started, a thread
-- that ended first had it cleared before the store, and the join waited forever
-- on a dead one (200000 rounds hung before round 20000).
assume os_bindung_klon
    "`gabbro_os_klon` stores a NONZERO value into the word at `wort` before its new thread can run and clears that word to 0 once the thread has ended, never the other way round; the clear happens-before what a waiter reads; and the root is called with `spitze` as its stack top, ending the THREAD and not the process when it returns."
    falsifier sonde_os_klon;

-- **The reservation reads as zero, and that is its own assumption** -- because
-- the bounded heap does NOT scrub. `laufzeit/arena_dyn.c` commits lazily and
-- stores nothing into a newly committed slot; a program reads a fresh slot and
-- expects zero. That holds for a private anonymous mapping and would not hold
-- for a binding that handed back recycled storage, which is why it is named
-- apart from the ABI: *a binding that reused a freed region would leave a
-- program reading another run's bytes, with every other line of this file
-- unchanged.*
assume os_bindung_null
    "The bytes `gabbro_os_reserve` answers, and every byte `gabbro_os_commit` makes writable, read as zero until the program writes them."
    falsifier sonde_os_null;

-- -- the report channel and the stop -----------------------------------------
--
-- The runtime has no words of its own, because printing is an OS call. A
-- fail-stop travels as a CODE and two numbers (`laufzeit/bindung.h` lists the
-- six codes) and this binding's body carries one sentence per code.
--
-- `gabbro_os_ende` NEVER RETURNS, and Gabbro has no way to say so -- so the
-- runtime writes every call site as if it might and keeps a fail-closed line
-- behind it. The assumption above is where the promise lives instead.
extern fn gabbro_os_melden(code : u32, a : u64, b : u64)
    effects { pure } costs <= 4096 ops;

extern fn gabbro_os_ende(code : u32)
    effects { pure } costs <= 4096 ops;

-- -- the bounded heap's storage ----------------------------------------------
--
-- `reserve` answers the base of `bytes` of reserved-but-not-committed address
-- space or 0; `commit` makes a page-aligned range of it readable and writable
-- and answers 0 on success; `seitengroesse` is the granularity the runtime
-- rounds to. A bounded heap needs no more than these three from an operating
-- system: the ceiling, the committed prefix and refuse-on-full are the
-- runtime's arithmetic (`laufzeit/arena_dyn.c`), and the binding never decides
-- where a page begins.
extern fn gabbro_os_reserve(bytes : u64) -> u64
    effects { pure } costs <= 100000 ops;

extern fn gabbro_os_commit(basis : u64, versatz : u64, bytes : u64) -> u32
    effects { pure } costs <= 100000 ops;

extern fn gabbro_os_seitengroesse() -> u64
    effects { pure } costs <= 4096 ops;

-- -- the generated driver's locks ---------------------------------------------
--
-- One blob per `lock`, owned by `<unit>.treiber.c` and passed as a number. The
-- driver takes and releases; the binding decides with WHAT, and fail-stops
-- itself if the primitive refuses -- there is no `else` at a lock the driver
-- must hold, and the words are on this side anyway.
--
-- **The `costs` is the primitive's own work and NOT the wait.** `held <= N ops`
-- is the Gabbro clause that bounds a critical section, and waiting bounds are
-- NOT CLAIMED (`grammatik/Grammatik/Zielsatz/Spec.lean`, the header's list). A
-- number here that pretended to cover contention would be the one kind of cost
-- clause this language refuses to write.
extern fn gabbro_os_sperre_init(s : u64)
    effects { pure } costs <= 4096 ops;

extern fn gabbro_os_sperre_nimm(s : u64)
    effects { pure } costs <= 4096 ops;

extern fn gabbro_os_sperre_gib(s : u64)
    effects { pure } costs <= 4096 ops;

-- -- the generated driver's threads -------------------------------------------
--
-- `start` takes the blob and the address of the root and answers 0 or an error;
-- `warte` joins it. The body owns the whole lifecycle, which is why a FAILED
-- start must leave a blob the driver may still wait on: the driver's error path
-- joins everything it started before the failure, and a blob that never
-- completed would hang the program instead of ending it with status 2.
extern fn gabbro_os_faden_start(f : u64, koerper : u64) -> u32
    effects { pure } costs <= 100000 ops;

extern fn gabbro_os_faden_warte(f : u64) -> u32
    effects { pure } costs <= 100000 ops;

-- -- the run-time `start`, which is a different thread ------------------------
--
-- A declared `concurrent` member is started once by the driver, before the
-- program runs; a `start { f };` STATEMENT is executed by a running Gabbro
-- function, on a stack the EMITTER laid out as a static region of the unit. So
-- these two take a stack top and a join word where the pair above takes a blob
-- the runtime owns (`laufzeit/bindung.h` says why).
--
-- **This is where the raw system call lives**, and it is the last one: `clone`
-- (56) on the handed stack, the thread's own `exit` (60) when the root returns,
-- and `futex` (202) for the wait. They leave no undefined symbol at all, which
-- is why `instrumente/pruefe-os-bindung.sh` counts SITES for them
-- (`MARKE_ROHRUF`) and not names -- a runtime that had hidden its Linux
-- dependency in assembly would pass a symbol stage untouched. The mark is 0 in
-- `laufzeit/` since these two bodies exist.
extern fn gabbro_os_klon(koerper : u64, spitze : u64, wort : u64) -> u32
    effects { pure } costs <= 100000 ops;

extern fn gabbro_os_wort_warte(wort : u64, erwartet : u32)
    effects { pure } costs <= 100000 ops;

}
